  <text>
      <body>
         <div>
            
            <div type="image">
               <figure></figure>
            </div>
            
            <div type="poem">
               <lg>
                  <l>New York, New York,......n.y. A city tingling in the cold. This city</l>
                  <l>never sticks. Winds creating a tunnel underneath the skyscrapers. At</l>
                  <l>times it hurts to breathe.</l>
                  <l>Watching Japanese television. Comedy shows from Japan Everything</l>
                  <l>with a wash of rice paper. Colors:from red to green:yellows into blue.</l>
                  <l>Calligraphy in sound and motion. Asian faces. Strange language. Who</l>
                  <l>knows what they are saying? Is this Tokyo?</l>
                  <l>Frantic humor/split images. Mass produced faces we can laugh at It</l>
                  <l>all comes back to me.........</l>
               </lg>
               
               <lg>
                  <l>Late night blue television. Tuning in for a quick fuck on the motor-</l>
                  <l>cycle.</l>
                  <l>We stay across from the Chelsea Hotel, in a loft with a great view of</l>
                  <l>watertowers. Fire escapes zig-zagging across the landscape.</l>
                  <l>I see the city through vertical lines. Cold metal slashing through</l>
                  <l>the cityscape. And people. Crazies in the all-night Have-a-Donut. Shop-</l>
                  <l>ping bags and twilight chatter. Sirens cutting holes in my brain.</l>
                  <l>A city always on the move. So many people you want to scream at times.</l>
                  <l>Prality comes in flashcubes of consciousness. You couldn't hold your</l>
                  <l>breath if you tried.......</l>
               </lg>
               
               <lg>
                  <l>Whispers drill holes in the midnight air. People in doorways eager</l>
                  <l>to talk. New York is the only place in the world were you can fall in</l>
                  <l>love with mannequins, he said.</l>
                  <l>Invisible people crowd corners. Datapanik in the year sero......</l>
                  <l>Subway stations deserted, like empty cages in a long forgotten zoo. The</l>
                  <l>crush of bodies during the days leaves you gasping for air. Everywhere</l>
                  <l>is the grasp for air....</l>
                  <l>This is what we saw.</l>
               </lg>
           
            </div>
         </div>
            
            <div>
               <div>
            
                  <p>      
                     There are three established clubs
                     in the city:CBGB's, Max's, and Hurrah
                     All week music. Admission is $4 to $6
                     depending on the day. Count on seeing
                     anyone. It's the crowd that can sway
                     your feelings. CB's is overrun with
                     tourists eager for a glimpse of "new
                     wave". You can't get out of your seat
                     so where's the fun? Max's is better,
                     although the sound isn't as good. Th-
                     ere's a coldness about it to me. Hur-
                     rah's is highly considered by people
                     in the city. Getting better.
                     Then there are the week-end clubs...
                     Mudd Club, Club 57, Club Hollywood...
                     Studio 10 and sometimes Trax. Studio
                     10 is a Yippie club in the west Vill-
                     age. Club 57 is gaining prominence---
                     strong shows, good atmosphere and go-
                     od fun. Admission is $4. One of the
                     places to go to.
                     Mudd Club is the most highly prais-
                     ed. With acoustics rumored as being
                     designed by the one and only Eno--- it
                     is new and rising fast. Set in the
                     Soho area it provides weekend entert-
                     ainment of the most novel kind. Cons-
                     tant choices.
                  </p>
                  
                  <p>
                     Clubs advertize in the newspapers:
                     Village Voice and Shoh Weekly. Other-
                     wise you see wall posters. few bands
                     print posters. New bands never do.
                     We in Seattle have relied on wall
                     posters like a great wall of informa¬
                     tion. But we have not had media acce-
                     ptance. The price you pay for being a
                     provincial city...
                     Poster artwork runs the gamut from
                     imaginative to boring. Too few of the
                     former, too much of the latter.
                     Opinions are worn on lapels in this
                     city. Store bought, profuse. Why make
                     them when you can buy them? The star-
                     kest difference here is the accepti-
                     bility of new wave.
                     You can hang out in the East Villa-
                     ge: Manic Panic (and talk to Snooky)..
                     Trash &amp; Vaudeville, Defiant pose... Or
                     to the West Village and the Bowery¬
                     Next Empire and Revenge, Bookstores..
                     Record stores—the best being Village
                     Oldies, where you can hear reggae on
                     the radio, and any band you care to
                     on the stereo. Even clothing if you're
                     just in from the 'burbs....
                     Visit Punk Mag or the Rocker. Find
                  </p>
                  
                  <p>
                     book stores full of fanzines. It's all
                     here.
                     A decent selection of local fanzin-
                     es (besides the big two). Esther too
                     eclectic, or too "in scene". The best
                     are "Beat It" and "No"-the latter be
                     ing crammed with the impact of eight
                     million images, impact, writing and ?
                     Find it. Lesser interesting ones inc-
                     lude: the in-joke "Skull Damage" for
                     affecionados exclusively; "Smegma'--
                     xerox and fun, and "2+2= 3 or 4 or 5
                     or 6 or 7 or 8 or 9" -done by Susan
                     Springfield of the Erasers. As bori-
                     ing as her band and as trite.
                     Everyone wears black leather. Youre
                     either punk or pop. Take it as you
                     like. Lots of clothes for the clothes
                     conscious. Lots of celebrities.
                     Like most towns the people are spl-
                     it. A and B lists of bands depend on
                     who you're talking to. Too many imit-
                     ations: of Patti, of the Heads, of our
                     boys from OHIO. Some are fun. The au-
                     diences are made up of old-timers,
                     those who are or have been in bands. A
                     few wide-eyed kids figuring they've
                     stumbled onto something BIG. Then th-   
                  </p>
                  
                  <p>
                     ere are the tourists.
                     Lots of bands are working on relea
                     sing vinyl It helps the CB's ()()
                     out albums (the next one should be on
                     the racks soon, and is worth picking
                     up-ED.) People from established ban-
                     ds will produce newer bands. It's im-
                     pressive.
                     There is a third generation gaining
                     strength in the club scene. Bands ()()
                     have watched the older ones form and
                     are now attempting to avoid the same
                     pitfalls. Knowledge is passed on. Vi-
                     sions continue to expand. Younger pe-
                     ople on the way up.....
                     Commercially New York is a ()()
                     Witness the strength of Blondie, eve-
                     ryone's role model. But equally as
                     strong is the knowledge of their dec-
                     ision and desire for money. The musi-
                     cians continue to be accessable, ()()
                     running into one can bring you ()()
                     contact with another.
                     New York, once the progeniter of
                     radio stations has for the most part
                     fallen into the disco pool. ()()
                     is moe MOR. WNEW-FM, the first to ()()
                     new wave a listen (because of well ()()
                  </p>
           
                  <p>
                     CONTINUED ON PAGE 9
                  </p>
